LESSON_DB.history.m4 = {
  0: {emoji:"🇫🇷", subtitle:"Grade 10 · The French Revolution",
    illusText:"Liberty, equality, fraternity — 1789 changed how the world thinks about rights.",
    hook:{title:"Why our Constitution starts with rights", body:"South Africa's Bill of Rights says everyone is equal before the law and has the right to dignity. That idea — that ordinary people, not kings, hold rights — was fought for in the streets of Paris in 1789. The <strong>French Revolution</strong> overthrew a king, spread the language of rights across the world, and also showed how a revolution can turn violent."},
    intro:"<strong>Causes</strong>: France's society was split into three <strong>Estates</strong> — clergy (1st), nobles (2nd) and everyone else (3rd, about 97% of people) who paid most of the taxes. The government was bankrupt after wars (including helping the American Revolution), bread prices soared after poor harvests, and <strong>Enlightenment</strong> thinkers (Rousseau, Voltaire, Montesquieu) questioned the divine right of kings. <strong>Key events</strong>: the Estates-General (May 1789); the Third Estate forms the National Assembly and takes the <strong>Tennis Court Oath</strong>; the <strong>storming of the Bastille</strong> (14 July 1789); the <strong>Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</strong> (August 1789); the Women's March on Versailles; Louis XVI executed (1793); the <strong>Reign of Terror</strong> under Robespierre (1793–94, about 17 000 officially executed); Napoleon takes power (1799). <strong>Legacy</strong>: the end of absolute monarchy, ideas of citizenship and rights, nationalism — and debates about who was left out: women (Olympe de Gouges wrote a Declaration of the Rights of Woman) and enslaved people (the Haitian Revolution, 1791–1804, led by Toussaint Louverture).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⚖️ Three Estates", body:"A tiny privileged group; the Third Estate paid the taxes."},
      {t:"teal", title:"💡 Enlightenment", body:"Reason, rights and the idea that power comes from the people."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔥 Revolution and Terror", body:"Rights were declared — then thousands were executed in the Terror."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Cause", body:"Give one economic cause of the revolution.", answer:"Government <strong>bankruptcy</strong> from war debts, or <strong>high bread prices</strong> after poor harvests."},
      {title:"Event", body:"Why is 14 July 1789 remembered?", answer:"Crowds <strong>stormed the Bastille</strong>, a royal prison and symbol of the king's power."},
      {title:"Legacy", body:"How did the revolution affect enslaved people?", answer:"Its ideas inspired the <strong>Haitian Revolution</strong>, the first successful revolt by enslaved people, creating Haiti in 1804."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying the revolution gave rights to everyone — women and enslaved people were largely excluded at first.","Mixing up the Estates (the Third Estate was the common people).","Ignoring the violence of the Terror when assessing the revolution."],
    callout:{title:"Source skills", body:"Political cartoons of the Third Estate carrying the clergy and nobles on its back are common exam sources — explain the message and its usefulness."},
    activity:"Compare three rights in the Declaration of the Rights of Man (1789) with South Africa's Bill of Rights (1996). Which groups does each protect?",
    activityAnswer:"Both protect equality before the law, freedom of expression and freedom from arbitrary arrest. The 1789 Declaration spoke of “men” and citizens and ignored women and enslaved people; SA's Bill of Rights explicitly protects everyone against discrimination on grounds including race, gender and sexual orientation.",
    examTip:"In essays, structure causes as long-term (social inequality, Enlightenment) and short-term (bankruptcy, bread prices, Estates-General).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Estates",d:"The three legal classes of French society"},{w:"Enlightenment",d:"18th-century movement stressing reason and rights"},{w:"Reign of Terror",d:"1793–94 period of mass executions"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The Third Estate was made up of:",opts:["The clergy","The nobles","Ordinary people","The king's family"],a:2,et:"About 97% of the population."},
      {q:"The Bastille was stormed in:",opts:["1776","1789","1799","1815"],a:1,et:"14 July — now France's national day."},
      {q:"Who led the Haitian Revolution?",opts:["Napoleon","Robespierre","Toussaint Louverture","Louis XVI"],a:2,et:"A formerly enslaved leader."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Inequality, bankruptcy and new ideas toppled the French monarchy — spreading the idea of rights, but also showing the dangers of revolutionary violence."},

  1: {emoji:"🛡️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Transformations in southern Africa after 1750",
    illusText:"The Zulu kingdom, the Mfecane debate and new states: Lesotho, Swazi, Ndebele.",
    hook:{title:"Where did Lesotho come from?", body:"Lesotho is a country completely surrounded by South Africa. It exists because, in the 1820s, a leader called <strong>Moshoeshoe</strong> gathered refugees on a flat-topped mountain, Thaba Bosiu, and built a kingdom by welcoming people rather than conquering them. Southern Africa was being reshaped — new kingdoms rose, others broke apart — and historians still argue about why."},
    intro:"In the late 1700s and early 1800s, southern Africa saw major political change. <strong>Causes</strong> historians discuss: population growth and drought (the Madlathule famine, about 1800), competition for land and cattle, and trade with the Portuguese at Delagoa Bay (ivory, later slaves). <strong>The Zulu kingdom</strong>: Dingiswayo (Mthethwa) and then <strong>Shaka</strong> (from about 1816) built a centralised state with age-regiments (amabutho), new fighting methods and a royal capital. <strong>New states</strong>: Moshoeshoe's <strong>Basotho</strong> (diplomacy, cattle loans — mafisa); the <strong>Swazi</strong> under Sobhuza; <strong>Mzilikazi's Ndebele</strong>, who moved north to present-day Zimbabwe. <strong>The Mfecane debate</strong>: the older view blamed Shaka for causing widespread wars and empty land (the “Mfecane”); historians such as Julian Cobbing (1988) argued this myth was used to justify white settlers taking land, and that slave raiding and colonial pressure played a big role. Today historians see many interlinked causes.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"👑 State-building", body:"Shaka, Moshoeshoe, Sobhuza and Mzilikazi built new kingdoms."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🌾 Many causes", body:"Drought, population, land, trade and colonial pressure."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔍 A debated history", body:"The “Mfecane” idea has been challenged as a settler myth."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Leadership", body:"How did Moshoeshoe build the Basotho nation?", answer:"Through <strong>diplomacy</strong>, a defensive mountain stronghold (Thaba Bosiu), welcoming refugees and <strong>lending cattle</strong> (mafisa) to build loyalty."},
      {title:"Historiography", body:"Why did some historians reject the “empty land” idea?", answer:"It was used to claim that white settlers took <strong>unoccupied</strong> land — evidence shows the land was occupied and the causes were complex."},
      {title:"Military", body:"What were amabutho?", answer:"<strong>Age-regiments</strong> of young men who served the king, used for war and labour."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Blaming one person (Shaka) for all the upheaval.","Treating the Mfecane as an agreed fact rather than a debate.","Forgetting the role of Delagoa Bay trade and colonial pressure."],
    callout:{title:"Multiple perspectives", body:"CAPS History wants you to compare interpretations — say who holds a view, what evidence supports it, and why it matters."},
    activity:"Make a table with four leaders (Shaka, Moshoeshoe, Sobhuza, Mzilikazi): where they ruled, how they built power, and what their kingdom became.",
    activityAnswer:"Shaka — Zulu kingdom, military centralisation; Moshoeshoe — Lesotho, diplomacy and refuge; Sobhuza — Swaziland/Eswatini, alliances and strategic retreat; Mzilikazi — Ndebele, moved through the Highveld to present-day Zimbabwe.",
    examTip:"When asked about the Mfecane, always mention that the term and its causes are debated.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Mfecane / Difaqane",d:"Term for upheaval in southern Africa in the early 1800s; its meaning is debated"},{w:"Amabutho",d:"Age-regiments"},{w:"Historiography",d:"How history has been written and interpreted"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Moshoeshoe's mountain stronghold was:",opts:["Isandlwana","Thaba Bosiu","Majuba","Kwa-Bulawayo"],a:1,et:"Never taken by force."},
      {q:"Mzilikazi's people eventually settled in present-day:",opts:["Lesotho","Zimbabwe","Namibia","Mozambique"],a:1,et:"The Ndebele kingdom at Bulawayo."},
      {q:"Cobbing argued the Mfecane idea:",opts:["Was fully accurate","Was a myth that helped justify land seizure","Applied only to Lesotho","Was invented by Shaka"],a:1,et:"A key debate."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Southern Africa was transformed by new kingdoms after 1750 — and how we explain that change is itself a historical debate."},

  2: {emoji:"🗺️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Colonial expansion after 1750",
    illusText:"Frontier wars, the Great Trek, Natal and the mineral revolution.",
    hook:{title:"Why is Makhanda a contested name?", body:"In 2018 Grahamstown was renamed <strong>Makhanda</strong>, after a Xhosa prophet and warrior who led an attack on the town in 1819. The town had been founded as a military post in a war against the Xhosa. Names, statues and borders across the Eastern Cape still carry the memory of a century of <strong>colonial expansion</strong> and resistance."},
    intro:"After 1750, colonial control spread from the Cape. <strong>The Eastern Cape frontier</strong>: nine wars of dispossession (1779–1879) between colonists and the Xhosa over land and cattle; the British took the Cape in 1795/1806 and settled about 4 000 British settlers in 1820. The <strong>Xhosa cattle-killing</strong> (1856–57), inspired by the prophecies of Nongqawuse, led to famine and the collapse of Xhosa independence. <strong>The Great Trek</strong> (from 1836): Dutch-speaking farmers (Voortrekkers) left the Cape over British rule, the ending of slavery and land shortages; they clashed with the Ndebele and the Zulu (Battle of Ncome/Blood River, 1838) and founded republics (Transvaal, Orange Free State). <strong>Natal</strong> became a British colony (1843); indentured Indian workers arrived from 1860 for the sugar plantations. <strong>The mineral revolution</strong>: diamonds at Kimberley (1867) and gold on the Witwatersrand (1886) drew British interest inland, created migrant labour and compounds, and set the stage for war.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⚔️ Frontier wars", body:"A century of conflict over land on the Eastern Cape frontier."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🐂 The Great Trek", body:"Voortrekkers moved inland and founded Boer republics."},
      {t:"gold", title:"💎 Minerals", body:"Diamonds and gold changed the economy and drew in British power."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Cause", body:"Give two reasons for the Great Trek.", answer:"Dislike of <strong>British rule</strong>, the <strong>abolition of slavery</strong> and its compensation, and <strong>land shortages</strong>."},
      {title:"Consequence", body:"What was the impact of the cattle-killing?", answer:"Famine killed tens of thousands; survivors became <strong>labourers on colonial farms</strong> and the British took Xhosa land."},
      {title:"Economy", body:"How did the discovery of gold change labour?", answer:"It created the <strong>migrant labour system</strong>, with men housed in compounds far from their families."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Telling the Great Trek as a Voortrekker-only story — African communities already lived on the land.","Forgetting indentured Indian labourers in Natal.","Treating the mineral discoveries as purely economic, not political."],
    callout:{title:"Perspective matters", body:"The same event — e.g. Blood River/Ncome — is remembered very differently by different communities. Name both views."},
    activity:"Find three place names in your province that were renamed after 1994. Research the old and new names and why they were changed.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary, e.g. Grahamstown → Makhanda, Pietersburg → Polokwane, Port Elizabeth → Gqeberha. Explanations should link the old name to colonial history and the new name to local or African history.",
    examTip:"For “explain the impact” questions, give impacts on different groups: colonists, African societies, workers.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Dispossession",d:"Taking away land or property"},{w:"Voortrekkers",d:"Dutch-speaking farmers who left the Cape from 1836"},{w:"Indentured labour",d:"Workers contracted for a fixed period, often in harsh conditions"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Diamonds were discovered at Kimberley in:",opts:["1836","1867","1886","1910"],a:1,et:"Gold followed on the Rand in 1886."},
      {q:"Indentured Indian workers came to Natal mainly to work on:",opts:["Gold mines","Sugar plantations","Railways only","Diamond fields"],a:1,et:"From 1860."},
      {q:"The Xhosa cattle-killing was linked to the prophet:",opts:["Nongqawuse","Makhanda","Shaka","Moshoeshoe"],a:0,et:"1856–57."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Frontier wars, the Trek, Natal's plantations and the mineral discoveries spread colonial control inland — at great cost to African societies."},

  3: {emoji:"🏛️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · The South African War and Union",
    illusText:"1899–1902: a war for gold, concentration camps, and a Union that excluded the majority.",
    hook:{title:"Not just a “Boer War”", body:"It used to be called the Anglo-Boer War, as if only two sides fought. But about 100 000 black South Africans served as scouts, wagon drivers and soldiers, and more than 20 000 black people died in separate concentration camps. That's why historians now call it the <strong>South African War</strong> — and why the peace that followed shaped the next 90 years."},
    intro:"<strong>Causes</strong>: British imperial ambition and control of the Witwatersrand gold; the rights of <em>uitlanders</em> (foreign miners) in the Transvaal; the failed <strong>Jameson Raid</strong> (1895–96); Boer determination to stay independent. <strong>The war (1899–1902)</strong>: early Boer victories and sieges (Mafikeng, Ladysmith, Kimberley); British counter-attack; then guerrilla warfare. The British used a <strong>scorched-earth policy</strong> (burning farms) and <strong>concentration camps</strong>: about 28 000 Boer women and children and at least 20 000 black people died. Emily Hobhouse exposed conditions in the camps. Sol Plaatje's diary records the siege of Mafikeng. <strong>Peace of Vereeniging</strong> (1902): the republics became British colonies; the question of black voting rights was postponed. <strong>Union of South Africa</strong> (1910): four colonies united; the vote was mostly limited to white men (except a limited non-racial franchise in the Cape). The 1913 <strong>Natives Land Act</strong> soon restricted black land ownership to about 7% of the country. In response, the <strong>SANNC</strong> (later the ANC) was formed in 1912.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⛏️ A war for gold", body:"Control of the Rand's gold was central to British aims."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⛺ The camps", body:"Scorched earth and concentration camps killed tens of thousands."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🗳️ Union for whom?", body:"1910 united white South Africa and excluded the black majority."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Causes", body:"Give one political and one economic cause.", answer:"Political: <strong>uitlander rights</strong> / the Jameson Raid. Economic: British desire to control <strong>Witwatersrand gold</strong>."},
      {title:"Experience", body:"Why do we call it the South African War?", answer:"Because <strong>black South Africans</strong> fought, worked and died in it too, not only Boers and Britons."},
      {title:"Consequence", body:"How did Union affect black South Africans?", answer:"They were largely <strong>excluded from the vote</strong>, and the 1913 Land Act soon took away land rights — leading to the formation of the SANNC."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Ignoring black participation and black concentration camps.","Saying the war was only about uitlander voting rights.","Forgetting that the Cape kept a limited non-racial franchise in 1910."],
    callout:{title:"Primary sources", body:"Emily Hobhouse's reports and Sol Plaatje's Mafeking Diary are useful sources — always ask who wrote it, why, and for whom."},
    activity:"Write a short diary entry (120 words) as a child in a concentration camp in 1901, using facts from this lesson.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. A strong entry includes accurate details: tents, shortages of food and clean water, disease (measles, typhoid), family members on commando or working as scouts, and the farm being burnt.",
    examTip:"In source questions, comment on reliability: when was it written, by whom, and what was their purpose?",
    keyterms:[{w:"Scorched earth",d:"Destroying farms and crops so the enemy can't use them"},{w:"Concentration camp",d:"Camp where civilians were held during the war"},{w:"Union",d:"The joining of four colonies into South Africa in 1910"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The South African War was fought from:",opts:["1880–1881","1899–1902","1910–1913","1914–1918"],a:1,et:"About two and a half years."},
      {q:"Who exposed conditions in the camps?",opts:["Cecil Rhodes","Emily Hobhouse","Paul Kruger","Lord Kitchener"],a:1,et:"A British welfare campaigner."},
      {q:"The SANNC (later ANC) was formed in:",opts:["1902","1910","1912","1948"],a:2,et:"In response to exclusion after Union."},
    ],
    conclusion:"The South African War was fought over gold and power, devastated civilians of all races, and led to a Union that shut the majority out of politics."},
};

// ─── Module 5: Grade 11 CAPS Topics ─────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.history.m5 = {
  0: {emoji:"🚩", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Communism in Russia, 1900–1940",
    illusText:"From tsar to Lenin to Stalin: revolution, five-year plans and terror.",
    hook:{title:"Why was there a red flag in our struggle?", body:"The South African Communist Party has been part of the ANC alliance for decades, and the red flag appeared at many anti-apartheid rallies. Its ideas came from a revolution in Russia in 1917 that promised land, bread and peace to workers and peasants — and then built one of the most brutal dictatorships of the 20th century. To understand both the hope and the horror, start with <strong>Russia</strong>."},
    intro:"<strong>Tsarist Russia</strong>: an autocracy under Tsar Nicholas II; most people were poor peasants; workers in new factories faced harsh conditions. The 1905 revolution forced small reforms. <strong>1917</strong>: World War I losses and food shortages led to the <strong>February Revolution</strong> (the tsar abdicated) and the <strong>October Revolution</strong>, in which Lenin's <strong>Bolsheviks</strong> seized power promising “Peace, Land and Bread”. <strong>Civil war</strong> (1918–21): Reds vs Whites; War Communism; then the <strong>New Economic Policy</strong> (1921) allowed some private trade. <strong>Stalin</strong> (from about 1928): <strong>Five-Year Plans</strong> for rapid industrialisation; <strong>collectivisation</strong> of farms — resistance and famine killed millions (including the Holodomor in Ukraine, 1932–33); the <strong>Great Terror</strong> (1936–38): show trials, purges and the gulag labour camps. Communism in theory: workers own the means of production; in practice: a one-party state with no free elections or free press.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"✊ 1917", body:"Two revolutions: the tsar falls, then the Bolsheviks take power."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🏭 Stalin's economy", body:"Five-Year Plans and forced collectivisation."},
      {t:"gold", title:"⛓️ Terror", body:"Purges, show trials and the gulag."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Slogan", body:"What did “Peace, Land and Bread” promise?", answer:"An end to the <strong>war</strong>, <strong>land for peasants</strong> and <strong>food</strong> for hungry workers."},
      {title:"Economy", body:"What was collectivisation?", answer:"Forcing peasants to merge their farms into <strong>state-controlled collective farms</strong>."},
      {title:"Cost", body:"Give one human cost of Stalin's rule.", answer:"<strong>Famine</strong> during collectivisation, or millions imprisoned and executed in the <strong>purges and gulags</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Mixing up the February and October revolutions.","Treating communist theory and Stalin's practice as the same thing.","Ignoring the famine caused by collectivisation."],
    callout:{title:"Compare ideologies", body:"This topic pairs with Capitalism in the USA — exam essays often ask you to compare the two systems in the 1930s."},
    activity:"Make a two-column table: what communism promised versus what Stalin's USSR delivered (land, work, freedom, equality).",
    activityAnswer:"Promised land to peasants → farms were collectivised; promised workers' power → one-party dictatorship; promised equality → a new privileged party elite; rapid industrial growth was real but came at huge human cost.",
    examTip:"Use dates and numbers carefully: 1917 (two revolutions), 1928 (first Five-Year Plan), 1936–38 (Great Terror).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Bolsheviks",d:"Lenin's revolutionary party"},{w:"Collectivisation",d:"Combining private farms into state farms"},{w:"Purge",d:"Removing opponents through arrest, exile or execution"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The Bolsheviks were led by:",opts:["Stalin","Lenin","Trotsky","Nicholas II"],a:1,et:"October 1917."},
      {q:"Stalin's plans for rapid industry were called:",opts:["New Deal","Five-Year Plans","NEP","Marshall Plan"],a:1,et:"From 1928."},
      {q:"The gulag was:",opts:["A collective farm","A system of labour camps","A political party","A secret police chief"],a:1,et:"Millions were imprisoned."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Russia's revolution promised equality but, under Stalin, produced rapid industry alongside famine, terror and dictatorship."},

  1: {emoji:"🗽", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Capitalism and the USA, 1900–1940",
    illusText:"Boom in the 1920s, the 1929 Crash, the Great Depression and the New Deal.",
    hook:{title:"From the JSE to Wall Street", body:"When share prices crash, pensions and jobs disappear — South Africans felt this in 2008 and again during COVID-19. The worst crash in modern history happened on Wall Street in October 1929. It turned America's “Roaring Twenties” into the <strong>Great Depression</strong>, with a quarter of workers jobless, and forced the government to rethink how <strong>capitalism</strong> should work."},
    intro:"<strong>Capitalism</strong>: private ownership, markets and profit. <strong>The 1920s boom</strong>: mass production (Ford's assembly line), consumer goods bought on credit, rising share prices and buying shares “on the margin” (with borrowed money). But wealth was unequal, farmers struggled, and many black Americans faced segregation. <strong>The Wall Street Crash</strong> (October 1929): panic selling wiped out savings; banks failed. <strong>The Great Depression</strong>: up to 25% unemployment, “Hoovervilles” (shanty towns), the Dust Bowl drought. President Hoover believed in limited government (laissez-faire). <strong>The New Deal</strong> (Franklin D. Roosevelt, from 1933): relief, recovery and reform — public works jobs, banking regulation, Social Security (pensions and unemployment benefits). Critics said it went too far (government interference) or not far enough. The Depression spread worldwide, including to South Africa, where gold helped the economy recover after 1932.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📈 Boom", body:"Mass production, credit and speculation."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📉 Crash and Depression", body:"1929 panic led to mass unemployment."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏗️ New Deal", body:"Government stepped in with jobs, regulation and welfare."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Cause", body:"Why was buying on the margin risky?", answer:"Investors used <strong>borrowed money</strong>; when prices fell they couldn't repay, causing panic selling and bank failures."},
      {title:"Response", body:"What were the “three Rs” of the New Deal?", answer:"<strong>Relief</strong>, <strong>recovery</strong> and <strong>reform</strong>."},
      {title:"Comparison", body:"How did Hoover and Roosevelt differ?", answer:"Hoover favoured <strong>limited government</strong>; Roosevelt used <strong>active government spending and regulation</strong>."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying the Crash alone caused the Depression — inequality, overproduction and bank failures mattered too.","Thinking the New Deal ended the Depression quickly (recovery was slow).","Forgetting that the boom didn't benefit everyone."],
    callout:{title:"SA link", body:"Today's SASSA grants and UIF echo New Deal ideas: the state providing a safety net."},
    activity:"List three New Deal programmes and compare each with a South African government programme today.",
    activityAnswer:"Examples: Civilian Conservation Corps / Works Progress Administration (public works jobs) → Expanded Public Works Programme; Social Security → SASSA old-age grants and UIF; bank regulation → the Reserve Bank's Prudential Authority.",
    examTip:"For comparison essays, use the same headings for the USA and USSR: economy, government, rights, living conditions.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Capitalism",d:"Economic system based on private ownership and markets"},{w:"Speculation",d:"Buying shares hoping to profit quickly from price rises"},{w:"New Deal",d:"Roosevelt's programmes to fight the Depression"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The Wall Street Crash happened in:",opts:["1919","1929","1933","1939"],a:1,et:"October 1929."},
      {q:"The New Deal was introduced by:",opts:["Hoover","Roosevelt","Ford","Wilson"],a:1,et:"From 1933."},
      {q:"Buying shares “on the margin” means using:",opts:["Cash savings","Borrowed money","Gold","Government grants"],a:1,et:"Risky when prices fall."},
    ],
    conclusion:"America's boom ended in crash and depression, and the New Deal changed the role of government in a capitalist economy."},

  2: {emoji:"🧬", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Ideas of race in the late 19th and 20th centuries",
    illusText:"“Scientific racism”, eugenics, Nazi Germany — and how science rejects race hierarchy.",
    hook:{title:"Sara Baartman comes home", body:"In 2002, the remains of <strong>Sara Baartman</strong>, a Khoikhoi woman displayed as a curiosity in Europe in the early 1800s, were returned from a Paris museum and buried in the Eastern Cape. Her story shows how pseudo-science was used to rank people by race — ideas that later justified genocide in Europe and apartheid at home."},
    intro:"In the 1800s, some scientists claimed humans were divided into biologically distinct races arranged in a hierarchy — so-called <strong>scientific racism</strong>, using skull measurements and false readings of Darwin (<strong>Social Darwinism</strong>). <strong>Eugenics</strong> aimed to “improve” populations by controlling who could have children; it led to forced sterilisation laws in the USA and elsewhere. These ideas justified colonialism and segregation. <strong>Nazi Germany</strong> (1933–45): Hitler's racial ideology targeted Jews, Roma, disabled people and others; the <strong>Nuremberg Laws</strong> (1935) removed Jewish citizenship; the <strong>Holocaust</strong> murdered about six million Jews. <strong>Australia</strong>: Aboriginal children were removed from families (the Stolen Generations). After 1945, <strong>UNESCO statements</strong> (1950s) rejected biological race hierarchy; genetics shows all humans are 99.9% identical and share recent African ancestry. Links to SA: race classification under the Population Registration Act (1950) used similar thinking.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📏 Pseudo-science", body:"False measurements were used to rank people."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚠️ Eugenics", body:"Controlling reproduction to “improve” a population."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧬 Modern science", body:"There is one human race; we share recent African ancestry."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Definition", body:"What was Social Darwinism?", answer:"The false application of <strong>“survival of the fittest”</strong> to human societies, used to justify inequality and conquest."},
      {title:"Law", body:"What did the Nuremberg Laws do?", answer:"They <strong>stripped Jews of German citizenship</strong> and banned marriages between Jews and non-Jews."},
      {title:"SA link", body:"Which apartheid law shows the influence of race thinking?", answer:"The <strong>Population Registration Act</strong> (1950), which classified everyone by race."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Treating race science as genuine science — it was pseudo-science.","Thinking these ideas were limited to Nazi Germany.","Forgetting the victims' voices and resistance."],
    callout:{title:"Handle with care", body:"This topic deals with real suffering. Use respectful language and focus on how ideas led to actions and laws."},
    activity:"Visit (online or in person) the Johannesburg Holocaust & Genocide Centre or the Apartheid Museum. Write down two connections between race thinking in Europe and in South Africa.",
    activityAnswer:"Examples: both used laws to classify people by race (Nuremberg Laws, Population Registration Act); both banned mixed marriages (Nuremberg Laws, Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act 1949); both used propaganda claiming one group was superior.",
    examTip:"In essays, trace a line from ideas (pseudo-science) → policies (laws) → consequences (persecution, genocide).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Eugenics",d:"Pseudo-science aimed at controlling human reproduction"},{w:"Holocaust",d:"Nazi genocide of about six million Jews"},{w:"Social Darwinism",d:"Misuse of evolution to justify inequality"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"About how many Jews were murdered in the Holocaust?",opts:["600 000","1 million","6 million","20 million"],a:2,et:"Along with millions of others."},
      {q:"Sara Baartman's remains were returned to SA in:",opts:["1994","2002","2010","1985"],a:1,et:"Buried in the Gamtoos Valley."},
      {q:"Modern genetics shows humans are about:",opts:["50% identical","90% identical","99.9% identical","Completely different by race"],a:2,et:"One human race."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Pseudo-scientific ideas about race justified colonialism, genocide and apartheid — and modern science has firmly rejected them."},

  3: {emoji:"🌍", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Nationalisms: South Africa, the Middle East and Africa",
    illusText:"African nationalism, Afrikaner nationalism, and nationalism in the Middle East and Ghana.",
    hook:{title:"Two nationalisms, one country", body:"In 1938 thousands of Afrikaners re-enacted the Great Trek with ox-wagons to celebrate its centenary. In 1955, thousands gathered at Kliptown to adopt the Freedom Charter. Both were <strong>nationalist</strong> movements in South Africa — one built an exclusive identity that led to apartheid, the other an inclusive vision of a free, non-racial South Africa."},
    intro:"<strong>Nationalism</strong> is the belief that a people sharing an identity should govern themselves. It can be <strong>inclusive</strong> (open to all who share a territory) or <strong>exclusive</strong> (defined by race, ethnicity or religion). <strong>Afrikaner nationalism</strong>: grew from the South African War, poverty (the “poor white” problem), language (Afrikaans recognised in 1925) and organisations like the Broederbond; the 1938 Trek centenary; the National Party won in 1948 and introduced apartheid. <strong>African nationalism</strong>: the SANNC/ANC (1912); the ANC Youth League (1944, Lembede, Mandela, Sisulu, Tambo) and the Programme of Action (1949); the Freedom Charter (1955); the PAC split (1959) with an Africanist vision. <strong>Middle East</strong>: Zionism (a Jewish homeland) and Arab/Palestinian nationalism clashed over Palestine; the state of Israel was created in 1948 and hundreds of thousands of Palestinians were displaced (the Nakba). <strong>Africa</strong>: in the Gold Coast, Kwame Nkrumah led a mass nationalist movement, and Ghana became the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence (1957).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🤝 Inclusive", body:"Freedom Charter: “South Africa belongs to all who live in it.”"},
      {t:"teal", title:"🚧 Exclusive", body:"Afrikaner nationalism was defined by language, culture and race."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🕊️ Other cases", body:"Ghana's independence; conflicting nationalisms over Palestine."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Compare", body:"How did the Freedom Charter's vision differ from Afrikaner nationalism?", answer:"The Charter was <strong>inclusive and non-racial</strong>; Afrikaner nationalism was <strong>exclusive</strong>, putting one group's interests first."},
      {title:"Leaders", body:"Who founded the ANC Youth League in 1944?", answer:"Leaders including <strong>Anton Lembede, Nelson Mandela, Walter Sisulu and Oliver Tambo</strong>."},
      {title:"Africa", body:"Why was 1957 important?", answer:"<strong>Ghana</strong> became independent under Nkrumah, inspiring independence movements across Africa."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Assuming nationalism is always positive or always negative.","Ignoring the PAC's Africanist view.","Treating the Middle East conflict as one-sided — present both nationalisms."],
    callout:{title:"Key quote", body:"Freedom Charter (1955): “South Africa belongs to all who live in it, black and white.”"},
    activity:"Draw a timeline from 1912 to 1960 showing at least four events in African nationalism and four in Afrikaner nationalism.",
    activityAnswer:"African: 1912 SANNC, 1944 Youth League, 1949 Programme of Action, 1952 Defiance Campaign, 1955 Freedom Charter, 1959 PAC. Afrikaner: 1918 Broederbond, 1925 Afrikaans official, 1938 Trek centenary, 1948 NP victory.",
    examTip:"Define the type of nationalism (inclusive/exclusive) before discussing a case study.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Nationalism",d:"Belief that a people should govern themselves"},{w:"Africanism",d:"Nationalism emphasising African self-reliance and leadership"},{w:"Nakba",d:"“Catastrophe” — the displacement of Palestinians in 1948"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The Freedom Charter was adopted at:",opts:["Sharpeville","Kliptown","Soweto","Bloemfontein"],a:1,et:"26 June 1955."},
      {q:"The National Party came to power in:",opts:["1910","1938","1948","1961"],a:2,et:"Then introduced apartheid."},
      {q:"The first sub-Saharan colony to gain independence was:",opts:["Kenya","Ghana","Nigeria","Tanzania"],a:1,et:"1957, under Nkrumah."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Nationalism shaped the 20th century: inclusive or exclusive, it built nations, freed colonies and also fuelled conflict."},
};

