LESSON_DB.history.m6 = {
  0: {emoji:"✊🏿", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Civil society protests, 1950s–1990s",
    illusText:"US civil rights, Black Power, women's and peace movements, and the anti-apartheid movement.",
    hook:{title:"From Montgomery to #FeesMustFall", body:"In 2015, students at Wits and UCT shut down campuses with #FeesMustFall and #RhodesMustFall. They used sit-ins, marches and boycotts — methods tested decades earlier by <strong>civil society</strong> movements around the world, from bus boycotts in Alabama to anti-apartheid protests in London."},
    intro:"<strong>Civil society</strong> means citizens organising outside government to bring change. <strong>US Civil Rights Movement</strong>: segregation (“Jim Crow”) in the South; the <strong>Montgomery Bus Boycott</strong> (1955–56) after Rosa Parks' arrest; Martin Luther King Jr's non-violence; sit-ins, Freedom Rides, the March on Washington (1963); the Civil Rights Act (1964) and Voting Rights Act (1965). <strong>Black Power</strong>: Malcolm X, the Black Panther Party — self-defence, black pride and community programmes. <strong>Women's movements</strong>: campaigns for equal pay and rights. <strong>Anti-war movement</strong>: protests against the Vietnam War. <strong>Anti-apartheid movement</strong>: international boycotts of South African goods and sport, disinvestment campaigns, and the “Free Mandela” campaigns. <strong>Environmental movement</strong>: growing from the 1970s. These movements used petitions, boycotts, marches, music and the media to put pressure on governments.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🚌 Non-violence", body:"Boycotts, sit-ins and marches to expose injustice."},
      {t:"teal", title:"✊ Black Power", body:"Pride, self-reliance and self-defence."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🌐 Global solidarity", body:"Boycotts and campaigns against apartheid worldwide."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Method", body:"Why was the Montgomery Bus Boycott effective?", answer:"Black passengers were most of the riders, so the boycott caused <strong>heavy financial losses</strong>; it ended with a court ruling against bus segregation."},
      {title:"Compare", body:"How did Malcolm X's approach differ from King's?", answer:"King stressed <strong>non-violence and integration</strong>; Malcolm X stressed <strong>black pride, self-defence and separatism</strong> (later softened)."},
      {title:"SA link", body:"How did civil society abroad pressure apartheid?", answer:"Through <strong>sports and cultural boycotts</strong>, consumer boycotts and <strong>disinvestment</strong> campaigns."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Presenting the civil rights movement as only Martin Luther King.","Ignoring women's leadership (Rosa Parks, Ella Baker, Fannie Lou Hamer).","Forgetting the international anti-apartheid movement."],
    callout:{title:"Source analysis", body:"Speeches like King's “I Have a Dream” (1963) are common sources — explain the message, the audience and the purpose."},
    activity:"Pick one recent South African protest (e.g. #FeesMustFall). Compare its aims and methods with the Montgomery Bus Boycott.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. E.g. #FeesMustFall aimed at free higher education and decolonised curricula, using shutdowns, marches and social media; Montgomery aimed to end bus segregation using a year-long boycott and legal action. Both used mass non-cooperation and media attention.",
    examTip:"For essays, give specific events with dates and explain how each method put pressure on the authorities.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Civil society",d:"Citizens organising outside government"},{w:"Boycott",d:"Refusing to buy, use or take part in something as protest"},{w:"Segregation",d:"Separating people by race by law or custom"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The Montgomery Bus Boycott began after the arrest of:",opts:["Martin Luther King","Rosa Parks","Malcolm X","Ella Baker"],a:1,et:"December 1955."},
      {q:"The US Civil Rights Act was passed in:",opts:["1955","1964","1968","1976"],a:1,et:"Followed by the Voting Rights Act in 1965."},
      {q:"Disinvestment means:",opts:["Investing more","Withdrawing investment as pressure","Printing money","Banning protests"],a:1,et:"A key anti-apartheid tactic."},
    ],
    conclusion:"From 1950 to 1990, civil society movements used organised, often non-violent action to win rights — and helped isolate apartheid South Africa."},

  1: {emoji:"📢", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Civil resistance in South Africa, 1970s–1980s",
    illusText:"Black Consciousness, Soweto 1976, the UDF and the states of emergency.",
    hook:{title:"“Black man, you are on your own”", body:"In the late 1960s, a young medical student named <strong>Steve Biko</strong> argued that black South Africans had to free their minds before they could free themselves. His <strong>Black Consciousness</strong> ideas inspired the learners who marched in Soweto in 1976 — and a generation that made South Africa ungovernable in the 1980s."},
    intro:"<strong>Black Consciousness (BC)</strong>: Steve Biko and SASO (1968) promoted black pride, self-reliance and psychological liberation; Biko died in police custody in 1977. <strong>Soweto Uprising</strong> (16 June 1976): learners protested against Afrikaans as a medium of instruction; police opened fire; Hector Pieterson was among those killed; the revolt spread nationally and many young people went into exile to join MK. <strong>1980s resistance</strong>: P.W. Botha's “total strategy” and the 1983 Tricameral Parliament (which excluded black Africans) provoked the <strong>United Democratic Front (UDF)</strong> (1983) — hundreds of civic, student, church and worker organisations. <strong>COSATU</strong> (1985) united trade unions. Tactics: rent and consumer boycotts, stayaways, street committees, “Release Mandela” campaigns. The state declared <strong>states of emergency</strong> (1985–90), detaining thousands. The <strong>End Conscription Campaign</strong> involved white youth refusing military service. Pressure from inside and outside the country pushed the government towards negotiations.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧠 Black Consciousness", body:"Psychological liberation and black pride."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🎒 1976", body:"Learners against Afrikaans and Bantu Education."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏘️ Mass democratic movement", body:"UDF and COSATU made the country ungovernable."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Cause", body:"What triggered the Soweto Uprising?", answer:"The decree making <strong>Afrikaans</strong> a compulsory medium of instruction, on top of anger at Bantu Education."},
      {title:"Organisation", body:"Why was the UDF formed in 1983?", answer:"To oppose the <strong>Tricameral Parliament</strong>, which gave limited representation to coloured and Indian South Africans and none to black Africans."},
      {title:"Repression", body:"What did the states of emergency allow?", answer:"<strong>Detention without trial</strong>, banning of gatherings and media restrictions."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Thinking 1976 was only about language — it was about Bantu Education and apartheid as a whole.","Forgetting the role of trade unions (COSATU).","Ignoring white resistance such as the End Conscription Campaign."],
    callout:{title:"Remember the date", body:"16 June is Youth Day — a public holiday honouring the learners of 1976."},
    activity:"Interview an older family member or community member about the 1980s (boycotts, stayaways, the states of emergency). Record three facts and one personal memory.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. Good answers include specific events (e.g. a consumer boycott in their town), dates, and how daily life was affected, and they compare the memory with what the textbook says.",
    examTip:"Link causes and effects across time: Bantu Education (1953) → Soweto (1976) → exile and MK → 1980s mass resistance.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Black Consciousness",d:"Movement for black pride and self-reliance"},{w:"UDF",d:"United Democratic Front, formed 1983"},{w:"State of emergency",d:"Special powers allowing detention and bans"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Black Consciousness is most associated with:",opts:["Oliver Tambo","Steve Biko","P.W. Botha","Chris Hani"],a:1,et:"Founder of SASO in 1968."},
      {q:"The UDF was formed in:",opts:["1976","1983","1990","1994"],a:1,et:"Against the Tricameral Parliament."},
      {q:"COSATU united:",opts:["Political parties","Trade unions","Churches","Student bodies only"],a:1,et:"Formed in 1985."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Black Consciousness, the 1976 generation and the mass movements of the 1980s made apartheid unworkable and opened the road to negotiations."},

  2: {emoji:"🌐", subtitle:"Grade 12 · The end of the Cold War and a new world order",
    illusText:"1989: the Berlin Wall falls; the USSR collapses in 1991; globalisation reshapes the world.",
    hook:{title:"Why your phone was made in five countries", body:"Your phone was probably designed in one country, assembled in another from parts made in several more, and sold everywhere. That's <strong>globalisation</strong> — and it took off after the Cold War ended. The fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989 also changed South Africa: without the “communist threat”, the apartheid government lost an excuse and began to negotiate."},
    intro:"<strong>The end of the Cold War</strong>: Mikhail Gorbachev's reforms in the USSR — <strong>glasnost</strong> (openness) and <strong>perestroika</strong> (economic restructuring) — loosened control; Eastern European countries broke away in 1989; the <strong>Berlin Wall</strong> fell on 9 November 1989; Germany reunited (1990); the <strong>USSR collapsed</strong> in December 1991. <strong>Impact on South Africa</strong>: the National Party could no longer present the ANC as a Soviet threat; Western allies pushed harder for reform; on 2 February 1990 F.W. de Klerk unbanned the liberation movements and Mandela was released. <strong>A new world order</strong>: the USA as the single superpower; <strong>globalisation</strong> — the rapid spread of trade, finance, technology and culture across borders, driven by multinational companies, institutions like the IMF, World Bank and WTO, and the internet. Debates: globalisation brought growth and cheaper goods, but also job losses, inequality between and within countries, and debts in the Global South. African responses include the African Union (2002) and NEPAD.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧱 1989–1991", body:"Wall falls, Eastern Europe breaks away, USSR collapses."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🇿🇦 SA impact", body:"No more “red threat” — the door to negotiations opened."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🌐 Globalisation", body:"Trade, finance and technology linked the world — unequally."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Reform", body:"What did glasnost mean?", answer:"<strong>Openness</strong> — more freedom of speech and criticism of the government."},
      {title:"SA link", body:"How did the end of the Cold War influence De Klerk?", answer:"With the <strong>Soviet threat gone</strong>, the West had less reason to support the NP, and the ANC lost Soviet backing — both sides moved towards talks."},
      {title:"Debate", body:"Give one argument for and one against globalisation.", answer:"For: <strong>growth, investment and cheaper goods</strong>. Against: <strong>job losses and widening inequality</strong>, especially in the Global South."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying the Berlin Wall fell in 1991 (it was 1989; the USSR ended in 1991).","Treating globalisation as only economic — it is also cultural and technological.","Ignoring Africa's place in the new world order."],
    callout:{title:"Essay link", body:"This topic connects to “The coming of democracy in SA” — show how international change made negotiations possible."},
    activity:"Check the labels on five items at home. List where each was made and explain what this shows about globalisation.",
    activityAnswer:"Answers vary. Items will often come from China, India, the EU and the USA. This shows global supply chains, trade agreements and cheaper production costs abroad — and raises questions about local manufacturing jobs.",
    examTip:"Use the key dates as anchors: 9 Nov 1989 (Wall), 2 Feb 1990 (De Klerk's speech), Dec 1991 (USSR dissolves).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Glasnost",d:"Gorbachev's policy of openness"},{w:"Perestroika",d:"Gorbachev's economic restructuring"},{w:"Globalisation",d:"Increasing worldwide connections in trade, culture and technology"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The Berlin Wall fell in:",opts:["1985","1989","1991","1994"],a:1,et:"9 November 1989."},
      {q:"Perestroika means:",opts:["Openness","Restructuring","Collectivisation","Isolation"],a:1,et:"Economic reform."},
      {q:"The USSR officially dissolved in:",opts:["1989","1990","1991","1993"],a:2,et:"December 1991."},
    ],
    conclusion:"The Cold War ended between 1989 and 1991, helping open the way for South Africa's negotiations and ushering in an age of globalisation."},
};

